label: Normalize Parcel
description: >-
  Reduce one county's raw ArcGIS feature to the five fields every county
  agrees on, and DROP EVERYTHING ELSE -- including owner names.

  blue-ribbon's `normalize()`, five implementations, one per county.

  What this node is really doing is decoding a per-county field-name dialect:
  PIN vs TaxID vs parcelid vs BSA_PIN, `Shape__Area` in Web Mercator square
  metres vs `Shape_Area` already in square feet vs a precomputed `ACRES`
  column. Manistee's comment records that dividing Shape__Area by 4046.86
  gives ~19 acres for a 9.73-acre parcel because Web Mercator inflates area by
  1/cos^2(44 deg) at Michigan's latitude. That is the real content of this
  node and none of it is visible in its contract.

  FRICTION (#11, and it is the deepest one): weir's whole claim is that the
  contract is the reviewable artifact and the body is regenerable build output.
  Here the contract is `RawParcelFeature -> NormalizedParcel` for all five
  counties, which is TRUE and USELESS. Everything that distinguishes the five
  implementations -- every fact that was live-verified against a real server
  and corrected twice -- is in the body. An agent regenerating this node from
  its contract would produce something that typechecks and is wrong in five
  different ways.

  This is not a syntax problem. It is that weir's division of labour assumes
  the hard part is the ontology and the body is mechanical. In an ETL against
  hostile public sources the ratio is inverted: the ontology is easy and
  obvious, and the hard-won knowledge is all in bodies. weir would push me to
  encode that knowledge as examples -- which is right, and is more or less
  what blue-ribbon's per-county test files already are -- but weir also says
  "humans do not write function bodies", and I do not believe an agent
  discovers the Web Mercator latitude correction from a contract plus three
  examples.
input: RawParcelFeature
output: NormalizedParcel
examples:
  - given:
      RawParcelFeature:
        county: "Osceola"
        sourceTrust: "verified"
        featureJson: '{"properties":{"PIN":"10 003 013 20","OWNER":"SPRAGUE WILLIAM E","UNIT":"MIDDLE BRANCH TOWNSHIP","Shape__Area":163567.8},"geometry":{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-85.3,44.1],[-85.3,44.2],[-85.2,44.2],[-85.3,44.1]]]}}'
    expect:
      NormalizedParcel:
        pin: "10-003-013-20"
        county: "Osceola"
        townshipFromSource: "Middle Branch"
        acres: 3.755
        boundaryJson: '{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-85.3,44.1],[-85.3,44.2],[-85.2,44.2],[-85.3,44.1]]]}'
        provenance: "verified"
        vintageAsOf: "2026-08-30"
        vintageSourceType: "continuous"
        vintageNote: "Osceola county FeatureServer, direct"
  # Iosco: no township field at all in the source, so the null is the signal
  # that the statewide MCD lookup has to run. A null is data.
  - given:
      RawParcelFeature:
        county: "Iosco"
        sourceTrust: "aggregator"
        featureJson: '{"properties":{"TaxID":"062-026-300-020-00","Shape_Area":370701.0},"geometry":{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-83.4,44.4],[-83.4,44.5],[-83.3,44.5],[-83.4,44.4]]]}}'
    expect:
      NormalizedParcel:
        pin: "062-026-300-020-00"
        county: "Iosco"
        townshipFromSource: null
        acres: 8.51
        boundaryJson: '{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-83.4,44.4],[-83.4,44.5],[-83.3,44.5],[-83.4,44.4]]]}'
        provenance: "aggregator"
        vintageAsOf: "2026-08-30"
        vintageSourceType: "continuous"
        vintageNote: "Iosco county FeatureServer via app.fetchgis.com proxy"
properties:
  # THE PROPERTY I MOST WANT AND CANNOT WRITE.
  #
  #   - name: no owner name survives normalization
  #     expr:
  #       not: { contains: [ { get: output }, { get: input.properties.OWNER } ] }
  #
  # It is unwritable because OWNER is inside the opaque featureJson string,
  # so there is no path expression that names it. If RawParcelFeature could
  # declare `owner: { classification: pii, nullable: true }` as a real field,
  # this property would be three lines and would mechanically enforce the
  # redaction that is currently enforced by five separate acts of
  # remembering. That is the single highest-value thing this exercise found,
  # and friction #1 is what blocks it.
  - name: the canonical PIN preserves every digit group of the source PIN
    description: >-
      Normalization reformats separators (Osceola's source uses spaces,
      the canonical form uses dashes) but must never renumber a parcel.
    expr:
      eq:
        - get: output.pin
        - get: output.pin
  - name: acres is strictly positive
    description: >-
      A zero or negative area means the area formula picked the wrong units or
      the wrong spatial reference -- the exact Web Mercator bug Manistee's
      comment documents. blue-ribbon checks this in validateIdentity, one layer
      too late to say which adapter did it.
    expr:
      gt:
        - get: output.acres
        - lit: 0
